gm-method/rendering (an instanced-LOD tree DISAPPEARS over a ~4m distance band at a LOD cutover and reappears past it -- not a 1-frame blink): ROOT CAUSE is two LOD levels SHARING one geometry OBJECT. @three.ez/instanced-mesh InstancedMesh2.addLevel reuses the level's child object when geometry===an existing level's geometry (objectsList.findIndex(e=>e.geometry===geometry)); the BVH-LOD cull then writes indexes[level][count[level]++] where indexes=levels.map(l=>l.object.instanceIndex.array), so two levels sharing an object share ONE backing array and ONE pair of desyncing counts -> they stomp each other's drawn slots and an active, fully-in-frustum instance lands in NO drawn bucket for the band around the cutover. The trigger: a LOD-geometry builder (meshopt simplify) returned the SOURCE geometry on a no-op/failure (e.g. MeshoptSimplifier.simplifySloppy missing or target-not-smaller), so the 'simplified' LOD geo === the LOD0 geo. FIX in your own code (durable, no node_modules edit): make the simplify helper ALWAYS return a NEW DISTINCT geometry -- clone on every fallback path, never return the bare source -- and clone any geo reused across a render LOD and the shadow LOD (addShadowLOD shares LODinfo.objects with addLOD). WITNESS live: read levels.map(l=>l.object.geometry.uuid) -- any duplicate UUID is the aliasing; after the fix all distinct and a per-instance bucket sweep (scan each level's instanceIndex.array[0..count] for the target index across a distance sweep) shows ZERO gaps. Note hysteresis and the impostor handoff are RED HERRINGS for this symptom (both verified clean); the aliasing is the cause.